        "original_id": "61109521-f4e6-5f0d-b2bd-85722e266496",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Brendan Bracken, Winston Churchill, Adolf Hitler\n- Organizations: Bank of England, British Conservative cabinet, Conservative Party, Labour Party\n- Places: United Kingdom, Ireland, Paddington North, Bournemouth\n- Events: World War II, Bournemouth by-election of 1945, 1945 Labour landslide\n- Concepts: Central banking\n\nFacts:\n- The Bank of England was established in 1694 and is the second oldest central bank still operating, after Sveriges Riksbank.\n- The Bank of England is the central bank of the United Kingdom and served as a model for most modern central banks.\n- Brendan Bracken was born in Ireland on 15 February 1901 and later became a businessman and a minister in the British Conservative cabinet.\n- Brendan Bracken is remembered for opposing the Bank of England's co-operation with Adolf Hitler and for supporting Winston Churchill's prosecution of World War II against Hitler.\n- In the 1945 Labour landslide, Brendan Bracken lost his Paddington North seat to Labour.\n- In the 1945 Bournemouth by-election, Conservative candidate Brendan Bracken won the seat and was described as a prominent supporter of Winston Churchill and a Conservative parliamentarian.\n- Brendan Bracken, 1st Viscount Bracken, died on 8 August 1958.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
